**`deploy.live-verify` and `test.fleet` are commands a person types, so there is nothing to unload.**
Neither writes anything, so neither leaves state behind. To retire `deploy.live-verify`, remove the
`check` row from `bin/harbor` and `contracts/cli.md`, then delete `bin/deploy-live-verify.sh` and its
suite; to retire `test.fleet`, remove the `test-fleet` subcommand from fleet.
Both were seen red on purpose before this row was written: a mispointed shim in a scratch
`HARBOR_SCOPE_LOCAL_BIN`, and a planted repo whose `just test` fails.

**`drift.reference-check` is the one row that is not scheduled and runs anyway** (the other
unscheduled rows are commands a human types), and it is here
because the constitution's rule is that every check and every notifier is
registered — not every *scheduled* one. It runs inline at SessionStart from
`session-verdicts.sh`, so it has no plist and `launchctl` cannot see it; to
disable it, remove section 6 of `hooks/session-verdicts/session-verdicts.sh` and re-run
`deploy-runtime-install.sh`. It is inline rather than scheduled because a full run is
0.31s against the 7s that moved `audit.triage` off the session path, and below that
threshold a cache stops being an optimisation and becomes a second failure mode:
one that has stopped being written reads exactly like a clean box.

REF-9 is nearly the whole of that number: **0.11s without it, 0.31s with**, the
two measured back to back on 2026-09-06 by stashing the rule and re-running. It
is one `awk` over 539 files rather than 539 invocations — the per-file form
measured 687ms and would have pushed this row onto a schedule. **This row said
`0.22s` until that measurement**, and nothing had changed to make it wrong: the
old figure was taken cold and re-reads as 0.11s warm. A timing typed into prose
is a copy like any other, and this one drifted 2x without a single edit.

**REF-10 and the denominator were measured in CPU, not wall-clock, and the
reason is worth keeping.** They landed while the box sat at load average 26 —
the self-hosted CI runner and three other sessions — where wall-clock said 1.9s
to 3.1s for the same binary and meant nothing. `user + system` is indifferent to
that: **0.97s before, 1.08s after**, three runs each, so the new rule costs
about 0.11s of CPU for a second `find` over the skills tree and one `sort -u`
into a file instead of a pipe. The `0.31s` above is idle-box wall-clock and is
**not** re-measurable under this load, so it stands unchanged rather than being
overwritten with a number taken through a different lens (CONSTITUTION No.2.7 —
an unavailable verifier means defer, never substitute).

**It went unregistered for its entire existence, which is the reason its row is
worth reading twice.** `bin/drift-reference-check.sh` was built 2026-08-03 and was in
no hook, no plist and no CI job — only in the handover how-to, as a command a human
types. Nothing ever typed it. Its REF-5 rule, whose whole subject is the
constitution's citations, also could not SEE them: the same commit that created
the tool migrated the constitution to a citation dialect the tool did not parse,
so it extracted 0 paths from a file carrying 10 dead ones and reported the
surface clean, with exit 0, for the 34 days it existed before anyone looked.

**THREE independently sufficient defects, not two.** Unregistered and unable to
fail were the first two. The third was found 2026-09-06 while answering "is it
running": `deploy-runtime-install.sh` on `origin/main` names neither `drift-reference-check.sh`
nor `doc-cited-paths.awk`, so the tool has **never been installed to the box at all**
— every `--live` run in its history was the repo copy pointed by hand at
`~/.claude`. A check absent from the machine it checks is the quietest of the
three, because the other two at least leave a file there to read (harbor#462).

**The `audit.triage` row watches TWO ledgers now, and the second one exists because the
first could not see harbor#462.** `guard-decisions.jsonl` records what the floor
DECIDED about a tool call and nothing about what the call then did — so a Read on
a path that resolves to nothing left no trace anywhere. Ten dead constitution
pointers were followed by sessions for 34 days; every follow was a tool call that
found nothing, and each session discovered it independently because none of them
could read what the last one hit. `tool-failures.jsonl` is written by
`failure-observe.sh` at `PostToolUseFailure` and judged by the same daily run,
under FC-10 (a failure recurring across sessions) and FC-11 (the recorder is
wired and has no sink, so every failure is being dropped).

**It registers and proposes. It never repairs**, and that is the constitution's
"Encoding a fix is a human-gated act" applied where the temptation is highest: a
ledger that quietly repaired the harbor#462 citations would have rewritten the
constitution on its own authority and gotten it wrong, because the correct repair
was to declare a path root, not to delete ten pointers.

**`status.usage` and `status.usage-sessions` run the same script** twenty minutes apart. One of
them is almost certainly dead weight, but "almost certainly" is not a reason to
delete a check, so both stay listed until someone reads the script.

## Exit codes (D-29, D-49)

Every check's status says one of five things, and a row's `red means` is written in these words:
**0** pass or skip, **1** it judged and found something, **2** a bad argument, **3** it could not
judge (unchecked: nothing was looked at), never a green and never a usage error. Before D-49 several
checks exited 2 for "could not judge", the code a usage error already had, so a caller could not tell
a typo from a blind run. The checks in this register that moved are `memory.route`, `lint.rule-health`,
`lint.port-paths` and `ci.main-tip-verdict`; `doc-visual-check.sh` (not scheduled) moved with them. Each has an arm
in its own suite for exit 3 blind and exit 2 on a bad argument. A reader of their status also accepts
the old 2 for a window, and `git grep -n 'D-49 window'` lists every such line. The contract and the
printed line are [`contracts/logging.md`](contracts/logging.md), "The summary line and the exit code".

A row whose check lives in another repo or in the private layer (the client monitors that defined
"exit 2 means … judge nothing") is that layer's to migrate: this file cannot see them.

A drift check also answers `<check> --status`: one row per item that is off, with desired, observed,
delta and how long it has been off, in the one shape of [`contracts/reconcile.md`](contracts/reconcile.md)
and with the exit codes above. It reports and never repairs. The checks that answer it are the
`standards/naming/controls.tsv` check rows whose script takes `--status`; this file does not keep that list,
`sh tests/drift/reconcile/contract.test.sh` derives it and reads each one.

## What is built and IDLE is registered too

This table answers "what runs on a schedule". It cannot answer the question that
`drift.reference-check` spent 34 days on the wrong side of — **what is built, asserts
something, and is wired to nothing** — because a tool nobody runs has no row to
be missing from. That complement is
[`contracts/idle-tools.txt`](contracts/idle-tools.txt), enforced in both
directions by lint rule **C8** (harbor#466): a tool with no caller must be
declared there, and a declaration for a tool that has since gained one is a
violation. Neither file is the other's index; together they are the whole
inventory, and "nothing runs this" stops being a state a tool can be in
silently.

## Status is DERIVED, never written here

This table carried a `status` column until 2026-09-04 and **two of its seven rows
were wrong** — a client monitor read "written, not yet loaded" while `launchctl`
had it loaded and `checks.log` already held one of its stamps, and `cu-doctor`
read "live, last exit 1" with prose below it calling that "the oldest unread red
on the box" while `cache/cu-doctor-state.json` said every gate green. Neither was
a lie when written. That is the point: a status typed into prose is a COPY, and
copies drift silently while still reading correctly — `CLAUDE.md`, "Don't state a
fact that lives somewhere else — derive it."

So the column is gone and the box answers instead:

```sh
launchctl list | grep com.harbor      # PID (- = not running), LAST EXIT, label
```

A label absent from that output is **not loaded**, whatever a plist on disk
suggests. Two traps this command does not show on its own:

- **A green out-of-band run does not clear a red schedule.** `launchctl` reports
  the last exit of the job it ran; a check re-run by hand afterwards writes its
  own receipt and leaves that exit code untouched. Read the check's receipt
  (`cache/cu-doctor-state.json` and friends) for what the gate currently says,
  and `launchctl` for whether the SCHEDULE is healthy — they answer different
  questions and can legitimately disagree.
- **`RunAtLoad` means a bootstrap is also a run.** A check re-loaded by hand
  fires immediately, so a fresh exit code may reflect that, not the schedule.

## Disable

```sh
launchctl bootout gui/$UID/com.harbor.<id>          # stop now
rm ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.harbor.<id>.plist     # stop for good
```

On Linux and WSL2, a row whose platforms say `macOS, Linux`. A unit's `<unit>` is the id with each dot a dash
(`audit.triage` is `harbor-audit-triage.timer`): a systemd unit name carries no dot before its suffix.

```sh
systemctl --user disable --now harbor-<unit>.timer    # stop now and for good
rm ~/.config/systemd/user/harbor-<unit>.service ~/.config/systemd/user/harbor-<unit>.timer
```

## Platforms

Every row above runs somewhere, and this table says where (harbor#617, epic harbor#831). The
platforms cell is one of four values: `macOS, Linux` (a launchd job on macOS and a systemd user
timer on Linux and WSL2), `macOS only` (with the reason), `macOS; Linux undecided` (a launchd job
nobody has yet decided to port), or `inherits: <host>` (no schedule of its own; it runs wherever its
host runs). `tests/check/schedules/schedules.test.sh` holds each `macOS, Linux` row's units in `systemd/` to
its plist — the same argv, the same schedule, the same run at start — and every row here to the
register, both ways.

**Installing a timer is the operator's, from a terminal**, for the reason the plists give: a
session that can stand up its own scheduled job can stand up anything. On WSL2, systemd must be on
first (`[boot]` / `systemd=true` in `/etc/wsl.conf`, then `wsl --shutdown`).

```sh
cp ~/port/harbor/systemd/harbor-<unit>.service ~/port/harbor/systemd/harbor-<unit>.timer ~/.config/systemd/user/
systemctl --user daemon-reload && systemctl --user enable --now harbor-<unit>.timer
systemctl --user start harbor-<unit>.service && journalctl --user -u harbor-<unit>.service -n 20   # see it run
```

A user timer stops at logout unless the user lingers (`loginctl enable-linger "$USER"`); launchd's
`LimitLoadToSessionType Aqua` jobs stop at logout too, so that is parity, not a gap.

